    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d548">

                <pb facs="0660" n="650" sameAs="frus:frus1942China/pg650"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M)/6125: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-05-04T20:00:00-04:00">May 4, 1942—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>354. Your mail despatch No. 330 March 24<anchor n="17a" xml:id="d548-f1"/>
                    reports 3305 tons tungsten tin and mercury and 500 tons wood oil ready for
                    shipment at points in Burma. Has Dr. Wong been able to effect transport of these
                    stocks to Myitkyina or to other places of safety and if not what is the
                    likelihood of saving such portion of them as have not been yet lost through
                    Japanese advance? Please report any available information.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
